@@@ problem 215 kth-largest-element-in-an-array
title: Kth Largest Element in an Array
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Divide and Conquer, Sorting, Heap (Priority Queue), Quickselect
params: nums, k
time: O(n) average
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=- unit=nums.length_(k_=_n/2)

=== description
Given an integer array `nums` and an integer `k`, return the `k`-th largest element in **sorted order** (not the `k`-th distinct element).

Can you solve it without sorting?

=== constraints
- `1 <= k <= nums.length <= 10^5`
- `-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4`

=== hints
- A min-heap of size `k` works in O(n log k).
- Quickselect: partition around a pivot, then continue only on the side that contains position `n - k`. Random pivots give O(n) on average.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::findKthLargest);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        return lc::lines(g.ints(n, -10000, 10000), n > 40 ? n / 2 : g.irange(1, n));
    });
}

=== approach
**Quickselect.** The answer is the element that would sit at index `target = n - k` after sorting. Partition the current range around a **random pivot** into three parts: `< pivot`, `== pivot`, `> pivot` (a three-way partition keeps it fast when there are many duplicates). If `target` falls in the middle part, the pivot is the answer; otherwise continue only in the part that contains `target`.

Each round discards a constant fraction on average, so the expected time is O(n + n/2 + n/4 + ...) = O(n), in place.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int target = nums.size() - k;              // index in ascending order
        int lo = 0, hi = (int)nums.size() - 1;
        mt19937 rng(12345);
        while (true) {
            int pivot = nums[lo + rng() % (hi - lo + 1)];
            // Dutch national flag partition of [lo, hi]:
            // [lo, lt) < pivot, [lt, i) == pivot, (gt, hi] > pivot
            int lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
            while (i <= gt) {
                if (nums[i] < pivot) swap(nums[i++], nums[lt++]);
                else if (nums[i] > pivot) swap(nums[i], nums[gt--]);
                else ++i;
            }
            if (target < lt) hi = lt - 1;          // answer among the smaller values
            else if (target > gt) lo = gt + 1;     // answer among the larger values
            else return pivot;                     // target lands on the pivot block
        }
    }
};

=== examples
[3,2,1,5,6,4]
2
> 5

[3,2,3,1,2,4,5,5,6]
4
> 4

=== tests
[1]
1
> 1

[2,1]
2
> 1

[7,7,7,7]
3
> 7

[-1,-1,2,9,-5]
1
> 9

@@@ problem 502 ipo
title: IPO
difficulty: Hard
topics: Array, Greedy, Sorting, Heap (Priority Queue)
params: k, w, profits, capital
time: O(n log n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..524288 time=nlogn space=n unit=number_of_projects_(k_=_n/2)

=== description
You start with capital `w` and may complete **at most `k`** distinct projects. Project `i` needs at least `capital[i]` to start and, when finished, adds its pure profit `profits[i]` to your capital.

Return the maximum capital you can end up with.

=== constraints
- `1 <= k <= 10^5`, `0 <= w <= 10^9`
- `1 <= n <= 10^5`
- `0 <= profits[i] <= 10^4`, `0 <= capital[i] <= 10^9`
- the answer fits in a 32-bit integer

=== hints
- At each step, among the projects you can afford, taking the most profitable one is never worse.
- Sort projects by required capital; as your capital grows, push newly affordable ones into a max-heap of profits.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int findMaximizedCapital(int k, int w, vector<int>& profits, vector<int>& capital) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::findMaximizedCapital);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        auto p = g.ints(n, 0, 10000);
        auto c = g.ints(n, 0, n <= 40 ? 20 : 5000LL * n);
        return lc::lines(n > 40 ? n / 2 : g.irange(1, n), g.irange(0, 10), p, c);
    });
}

=== approach
**Greedy with a max-heap.** Profits are never negative, so whenever you can do a project, doing the most profitable affordable one first only increases your capital and never hurts future choices.

Sort projects by required capital. Repeat up to `k` times: move every project whose capital requirement is `<= w` into a **max-heap** of profits (a pointer walks the sorted list, so each project is added once); if the heap is empty you are stuck; otherwise pop the largest profit and add it to `w`.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int findMaximizedCapital(int k, int w, vector<int>& profits, vector<int>& capital) {
        int n = profits.size();
        vector<pair<int, int>> projects(n);            // (capital needed, profit)
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) projects[i] = {capital[i], profits[i]};
        sort(projects.begin(), projects.end());

        priority_queue<int> affordable;                // profits of projects we can start
        long long cap = w;
        int next = 0;
        while (k-- > 0) {
            while (next < n && projects[next].first <= cap) affordable.push(projects[next++].second);
            if (affordable.empty()) break;             // nothing we can afford
            cap += affordable.top();                   // take the most profitable one
            affordable.pop();
        }
        return (int)cap;
    }
};

=== examples
2
0
[1,2,3]
[0,1,1]
> 4
: With capital 0 only project 0 is possible (profit 1). Then, with capital 1, take project 2 (profit 3): 4.

3
0
[1,2,3]
[0,1,2]
> 6

=== tests
1
0
[1,2,3]
[1,1,2]
> 0

10
0
[1,2,3]
[0,1,2]
> 6

1
2
[1,2,3]
[1,1,2]
> 5

@@@ problem 373 find-k-pairs-with-smallest-sums
title: Find K Pairs with Smallest Sums
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Heap (Priority Queue)
params: nums1, nums2, k
time: O(k log k)
space: O(k)
bench: n=64..262144 time=nlogn space=n unit=k_=_array_length

=== description
You are given two ascending arrays `nums1` and `nums2` and an integer `k`. A pair `(u, v)` takes one element from each array.

Return the `k` pairs with the **smallest sums**. When several pairs tie, any of them may be chosen, so the judge checks that your pairs are valid, distinct index pairs and prints the sorted list of their sums.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums1.length, nums2.length <= 10^5`
- `-10^9 <= nums1[i], nums2[i] <= 10^9`
- both arrays are sorted ascending
- `1 <= k <= 10^4`, `k <= nums1.length * nums2.length`

=== hints
- The smallest pair is `(0, 0)`. After taking `(i, j)`, the next candidates are `(i + 1, j)` and `(i, j + 1)`.
- Seed a min-heap with `(i, 0)` for the first `k` values of `i`; after popping `(i, j)` push `(i, j + 1)`. Each pair is then generated exactly once.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> kSmallestPairs(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2, int k) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        auto a = c.arg<std::vector<int>>(), b = c.arg<std::vector<int>>();
        int k = c.arg<int>();
        auto A = a, B = b;
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> r;
        Solution s;
        c.time([&] { r = s.kSmallestPairs(a, b, k); });
        // validate: every pair uses values from the arrays, no index pair is used twice
        std::map<int, int> ca, cb;
        for (int x : A) ca[x]++;
        for (int x : B) cb[x]++;
        std::map<std::pair<int, int>, long long> used;
        std::vector<long long> sums;
        for (auto &p : r) {
            if (p.size() != 2 || !ca.count(p[0]) || !cb.count(p[1]) ||
                ++used[{p[0], p[1]}] > (long long)ca[p[0]] * cb[p[1]]) {
                c.out(r);
                return;
            }
            sums.push_back((long long)p[0] + p[1]);
        }
        std::sort(sums.begin(), sums.end());
        std::string o = "\"pair sums [";
        for (size_t i = 0; i < sums.size(); ++i) o += (i ? "," : "") + std::to_string(sums[i]);
        c.out_text(o + "]\"");
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        int la = n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, 8) : n, lb = n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, 8) : n;
        int k = n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, la * lb) : n;
        return lc::lines(g.sorted_ints(la, -20, 20), g.sorted_ints(lb, -20, 20), k);
    });
}

=== approach
Think of the sums as a matrix `M[i][j] = nums1[i] + nums2[j]`: each row and each column is sorted. That is like merging sorted rows with a **min-heap**:

1. push `(i, 0)` for `i < min(k, m)`: the first element of each relevant row;
2. `k` times: pop the smallest `(i, j)`, output it, and push its right neighbour `(i, j + 1)`.

Each pair enters the heap at most once and the heap holds at most `k` entries: O(k log k).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> kSmallestPairs(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2, int k) {
        using T = tuple<long long, int, int>;                 // (sum, i, j)
        priority_queue<T, vector<T>, greater<T>> heap;       // min-heap by sum
        int m = nums1.size(), n = nums2.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < min(k, m); ++i) heap.emplace((long long)nums1[i] + nums2[0], i, 0);
        vector<vector<int>> out;
        while (k-- > 0 && !heap.empty()) {
            auto [sum, i, j] = heap.top();
            heap.pop();
            out.push_back({nums1[i], nums2[j]});
            if (j + 1 < n) heap.emplace((long long)nums1[i] + nums2[j + 1], i, j + 1);   // next in row i
        }
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,7,11]
[2,4,6]
3
> "pair sums [3,5,7]"
: The pairs are [1,2], [1,4], [1,6].

[1,1,2]
[1,2,3]
2
> "pair sums [2,2]"
: The pairs are [1,1] and [1,1] (using the two different 1s of nums1).

=== tests
[1,2]
[3]
3
> "pair sums [4,5]"

[-5,0,5]
[-5,0,5]
9
> "pair sums [-10,-5,-5,0,0,0,5,5,10]"

[1]
[1]
1
> "pair sums [2]"

@@@ problem 295 find-median-from-data-stream
title: Find Median from Data Stream
difficulty: Hard
topics: Two Pointers, Design, Sorting, Heap (Priority Queue), Data Stream
params: operations, arguments
time: O(log n) per addNum, O(1) per findMedian
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..524288 time=nlogn space=n unit=number_of_operations

=== description
The **median** is the middle value of an ordered list, or the mean of the two middle values if the list has even length.

Implement `MedianFinder`:

- `MedianFinder()` initializes the object.
- `void addNum(int num)` adds a number from the data stream.
- `double findMedian()` returns the median of all numbers so far (answers within `10^-5` are accepted). It is only called after at least one `addNum`.

=== constraints
- `-10^5 <= num <= 10^5`
- at most `5 * 10^4` calls in total

=== hints
- Keep the smaller half in a max-heap and the larger half in a min-heap.
- Keep their sizes equal or the max-heap one bigger; then the median is at the tops.

=== template
class MedianFinder {
public:
    MedianFinder() {

    }

    void addNum(int num) {

    }

    double findMedian() {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.design<MedianFinder>()
        .method("addNum", &MedianFinder::addNum)
        .method("findMedian", &MedianFinder::findMedian);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        lc::Ops ops;
        ops.add("MedianFinder");
        ops.add("addNum", g.irange(-100000, 100000));
        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
            if (g.coin(0.6)) ops.add("addNum", g.irange(-100000, 100000));
            else ops.add("findMedian");
        }
        ops.add("findMedian");
        return ops.lines();
    });
}

=== approach
Split the numbers into two halves:

- `low`: a **max-heap** with the smaller half;
- `high`: a **min-heap** with the larger half.

Invariant: every number in `low` is `<=` every number in `high`, and `low` has the same size as `high` or one more. To add a number, push it into `low`, move `low`'s maximum to `high` (restoring the order), and if `high` became larger move its minimum back. The median is `low.top()` for odd counts, or the average of both tops.

=== solution
class MedianFinder {
    priority_queue<int> low;                              // max-heap: smaller half
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> high;  // min-heap: larger half

public:
    MedianFinder() {}

    void addNum(int num) {
        low.push(num);
        high.push(low.top());          // largest of the small half moves up
        low.pop();
        if (high.size() > low.size()) {// keep low as big as high (or one bigger)
            low.push(high.top());
            high.pop();
        }
    }

    double findMedian() {
        if (low.size() > high.size()) return low.top();
        return ((long long)low.top() + high.top()) / 2.0;
    }
};

=== examples
["MedianFinder","addNum","addNum","findMedian","addNum","findMedian"]
[[],[1],[2],[],[3],[]]
> [null,null,null,1.50000,null,2.00000]

=== tests
["MedianFinder","addNum","findMedian"]
[[],[-7],[]]
> [null,null,-7.00000]

["MedianFinder","addNum","addNum","addNum","addNum","findMedian","addNum","findMedian"]
[[],[5],[5],[1],[9],[],[-3],[]]
> [null,null,null,null,null,5.00000,null,5.00000]

["MedianFinder","addNum","addNum","findMedian"]
[[],[100000],[-100000],[]]
> [null,null,null,0.00000]
